agent_trace.text:
- Short-term reversal following abnormally low-volume days in small-cap stocks predicts higher next-day returns.
- Long-term reversal after high-volume days in small-cap stocks predicts lower returns over the next 10 trading days.
- High-volume days in small-cap stocks predict higher returns when they occur near earnings announcements.

RECIPES
- AXIS: ORDER STATISTICS — the position of the highest volume day within the last 30 days, divided by the number of days with volume above the 75th percentile.
- AXIS: TIMESCALE CONTRAST — the difference between the 1-day and 10-day rolling standard deviation of returns for small-cap stocks with high volume.
- AXIS: RESIDUAL — regress $return on $volume and use the residual as a signal for the next period.
- AXIS: UNUSUAL PAIRING — compute the correlation between $high and $volume over the past 20 days, then rank by the absolute value.
- AXIS: INVERSION — take one minus the ratio of $volume to $open on high-volume days, and rank across stocks.
- AXIS: DEPTH — first calculate the 5-day moving average of $volume, then rank stocks by the percentage change from that average, then apply a z-score to those ranks.
- AXIS: ASYMMETRY — condition on whether $return is positive or negative on high-volume days, and compute separate z-scores for each case.
- AXIS: NEGLECTED OPERATOR — use DECAYLINEAR($volume, 10) and compare it to the median $volume over the same period.
- AXIS: CROSS-FAMILY PRODUCT — multiply the rank of $volume by the rank of $return for small-cap stocks, then normalize by the standard deviation of $return.

